  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Light rain north, scattered thunderstorms \nsoutheast today\n\nAn ill-defined front will continue to drift into Central PA this\nafternoon, bringing some warm advection showers across the\nnorthern tier. Although showers are expected to weaken with\neastward extent as they encounter some drier air, the radar\npresentation is showing no signs of weakening and light rain is\nbecoming increasingly like along and north of I-80 through the\nafternoon.\n\nMeanwhile, we are starting to see some convection initiation in\nthe Lower Susquehanna Valley. 500 to 750 J/kg of MLCAPE will\nprovide sufficient fuel for a few taller showers to develop and\nperhaps some lightning. As we head into the overnight hours, a\nwave of energy drifting across the Great Lakes will bring\nmore widespread rain chances into the overnight hours. All in\nall, rainfall totals north of I-80 will be between a quarter and\nhalf inch, with some locally higher amounts possible. Farther\nsouth, up to a quarter inch is possible but less than a tenth of\nan inch is most likely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Late summer heat+humidity to close August and \nopen September\n\nStrong upper ridge expanding east-northeastward from the \nsouth central Plains through the TN Valley into the southern \nMid Atlantic and Southeast states will deliver a stretch of \nabove normal late summer warmth and very humid conditions to \nclose out August and kickoff September. Forecast max/min \ntemperatures are projected to rise several degrees above the \nhistorical average Sunday-Thursday with the largest departure \nfrom normal (+10-20F) found in midweek overnight lows. The late \nsummer heat is forecast to peak Tuesday and Wednesday with max \nheat indices approaching advisory levels in the mid to upper 90s\nacross parts of south central PA. The well above normal \ntemperatures combined with high humidity and very warm overnight\nlows will result in level 2 (moderate) to level 3 (major) \nHeatRisk values Tuesday and Wednesday. Increasing troughing \nacross the Northeast U.S. should drop temperatures back toward \nseasonal climo by Friday/Saturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Bouts of heavy rain and severe storms possible\nTuesday-Thursday\n\nA series of mid level impulses tracking eastward along the \nnorthern periphery of the elongated 500mb ridge will interact \nwith anomalous moisture/instability and strong flow aloft to \nproduce a few episodes of heavy rainfall and possible severe \nweather through late week. There are typical timing and \namplitude differences of the smaller scale disturbances that \nwill need to be resolved, but the overall convective signal from\noperational/ensemble/ML/AI guidance is supportively bullish. \nThere is a growing signal for severe storms and heavy rain on \nTuesday through Thursday. \n\nSPC introduced a level 2 severe t-storm risk for D3/Tuesday and\nD5/Thursday and maintained the 15% or level 2 equivalent risk \nfor D4/Wednesday. WPC continues to cover parts of CPA with level\n1 excessive rainfall outlooks which may be refined/upgraded as \nthe convective details become more clear and confidence in \ntiming and location increases. The environment will be\ncharacterized by impressive CAPE (on the order of 2-3k J/kg) and\nsufficient shear. A corridor of strong storms is likely\nsomewhere across upstate NY into PA each day through the week,\nwith the initiating boundary each day being highly dependent on\nthe extent/intensity of storms the day before. The battleground between\na hot/humid/dry day and perhaps multiple rounds of strong \nthunderstorms will likely be draped somewhere across Central PA\nthis week, so those with outdoor plans should continue to\nmonitor the forecast closely.",
